            <div xml:id="d957">

                <pb facs="1093" n="981" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg981"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611B.9417/20a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-16T20:00:00-04:00">July 16, 1935—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>109. Your telegram 151, July 16, 4 p.m., last paragraph.<anchor n="64"
                        xml:id="d957-f1"/> Department suggests, provided that you perceive no
                    objection, that you casually express the hope that present negotiations between
                    the United States and Japan looking toward an apportionment of the Philippine
                    textile trade may be brought to a mutually satisfactory conclusion.</p>
                <p>With the exception of the foregoing, the Department would prefer to leave to your
                    judgment selection of other matters which might appropriately be discussed with
                    the Foreign Minister.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
